#Disease Background
Spinaland Bulbar Muscular Atrophy (SBMA), or  Kennedy disease, is an inherited disorder that mainly affects males and causes progressive weakness and wasting of the face, throat, arms and legs. It results from the
degeneration of lower motor neurons inn the spinal cord and brainstem (La Spada, 2026;Tanaka et al., 2012).

SBMA is caused by expanded CAG repeats in exon 1 of the AR gene at Xq12. The expansion produces an abnormally long polyglutamine region in the  androgen receptor which can accumulate in the nucleus and contribute
to motor neuron damage (La Spada et al., 1992; Belikov et al., 2015).

#Gene and Normal Protein
The official gene symbol is AR, which stands for androgen receptor. The gene is located on the X chromosome at Xq12 and provides instructions for making the androgen receptor protein. This protein receives signals 
from male sex hormones and helps regulate the activity of specific genes. It can be found in both the cytoplasm and nucleus and is involved in hormone signaling and gene regulation.

#Documented Mutation
The documented mutation used in this activity was:NM_000044.6(AR):c.2296G>A (p.Ala766Thr)

This mutation involves the substitution of one nucleotide, changing G to A at c.2296 in the AR coding sequence. The nucleotide change results in an amino-acid change from alanine to threonine at position 766. 
Because only one nucleotide was substituted, the reading frame remained unchanged and the mutation was classified as a missense mutation. The predicted protein remained 920 amino acids long.

#Hypothesis
If a mutation is introduced into the normal AR coding sequence, it is expected to affect the AR protein depending on the type and location of the mutation. The documented c.2296G>A mutation is expected to change 
alanine to threonine while keeping the reading frame and protein length unchanged. For comparison, the artificial GAA→GCA mutation is expected to change glutamic acid to alanine without causing a frameshift. 
These changes may affect the normal function of the androgen receptor.

#Methods
The normal AR coding sequence from NM_000044.6 was obtained and uploaded to Galaxy as the Wild Type sequence. The WT sequence was translated using the Transeq tool to obtain the predicted androgen receptor 
protein. The sequence and protein length, reading frame, start and stop codons, and amino-acid sequence were recorded.

For the documented mutation, a copy of the WT AR coding sequence was used. The G nucleotide at c.2296 was changed to A to create the c.2296G>A mutation. The mutated sequence was then translated using Transeq 
and compared with the WT protein to determine the amino-acid change, reading frame, protein length, and presence of a premature stop codon.

For the artificial mutation, one nucleotide was changed by modifying GAA to GCA at coding sequence position 5. The artificial mutant sequence was translated using Transeq and compared with the WT and documented 
mutant sequences. The sequences were compared based on mutation type, amino-acid changes, reading frame, protein length, and premature stop codon.

#Results
The WT AR coding sequence contained 2,763 bp and produced a predicted protein containing 920 amino acids. It began with the ATG start codon, ended with the TGA stop codon, and was translated in Frame 1.

For the documented mutation, the CDS remained 2,763 bp, and the predicted protein remained 920 amino acids. The first amino-acid change occurred at position 766, where alanine was replaced by threonine. The 
reading frame remained unchanged, and no premature stop codon was produced.

The artificial mutation changed GAA to GCA at coding position 5. This substitution was expected to change the second amino acid from glutamic acid to alanine. Because only one nucleotide was changed, the reading 
frame and overall protein length remained unchanged.

