The WT and documented mutant proteins remained the same until amino acid 766, where the alanine-to-threonine substitution occurred. No additional changes were observed in the downstream amino-acid sequence, and 
the mutation did not shorten the protein or alter its reading frame.

#Artificial Mutation Experiment
For the second experiment, an artificial mutation was introduced by changing GAA to GCA at coding position 5. This single-base change was expected to replace glutamic acid (E) with alanine (A) at the second 
amino-acid position.

Because the mutation involved only one nucleotide, the reading frame was not expected to shift. The resulting protein was therefore expected to have the same overall length, with a difference only at the affected 
amino-acid position.

#Interpretation
AR c.2296G>A → G is replaced by A → alanine 766 changes to threonine → missense mutation → reading frame remains unchanged → 920-aa protein → possible change in AR function → possible disruption of androgen 
signaling → androgen resistance.

The c.2296G>A variant changes one base in the AR coding sequence, resulting in the substitution of alanine with threonine at amino-acid position 766. The Galaxy results showed that the mutation did not cause a 
frameshift, premature stop codon, or change in protein length. Because the substituted amino acids have different chemical properties, the change may affect the function of the androgen receptor. Changes in AR 
function may interfere with normal hormone signaling and gene regulation.

#Limitations
This experiment relied mainly on DNA sequence analysis and protein translation through Galaxy. Although the results showed the nucleotide substitution, amino-acid change, reading frame, and predicted protein 
length, they could not directly determine the actual structural or functional effect of the mutation.

The artificial mutation was also created only as a controlled comparison and was not treated as a disease-causing variant. Additional laboratory studies would be needed to determine how these mutations affect 
the androgen receptor in living cells.

#Conclusion
The experiment demonstrated how a single change in a DNA sequence can result in a change in the protein sequence. The documented AR c.2296G>A mutation changed alanine at position 766 to threonine, while the 
reading frame and 920-amino-acid protein length remained unchanged.

The artificial mutation, GAA→GCA, produced a different amino-acid substitution, changing glutamic acid at position 2 to alanine. Both mutations were missense substitutions that did not affect the reading 
frame or overall protein length, showing that different single-nucleotide changes can produce different amino-acid changes and potentially affect protein function in different ways.

